# SYNOPSIS

```usage
log [-f]
```

# DESCRIPTION

`log` prints the system's log: the kernel's and every process's records, a line each as the console shows them, `[seconds.ms] pid:tid> text`.
It starts from the oldest line console(4) keeps, the last 64 KiB, and then prints each record as it comes, until it is interrupted.
`-f` is accepted and changes nothing: the log always follows.

It is an rc script, `cat /dev/kprint`.

# EXAMPLES

In a cpu session, the node's log on the dev machine's terminal; ^C ends it:

```rc
% log
```

# FILES

: `/dev/kprint`
  The log as text, console(4)'s `kprint`.

# SEE ALSO

console(4), cpu(8)

# BUGS

There is no way to print only what is kept and stop.
A reader too slow for the log skips what console(4) has stopped keeping.
